# What i learnt from the Data Download Guide-

What is API-
Short for Application Programming Interface API is the waiter that takes 
the client's (you) request to the kitchen(the server) and fetches the 
data to your device.

The Request: 
Your application initiates an API call to ask for specific information. 
This is done using formatted URLs called Endpoints combined with HTTP 
methods like GET (to fetch data) or POST (to send data).

The Security Check: 
Most platforms require an API Key (a unique digital passport) to verify 
who you are and prevent spam.

The Response: 
The remote server processes the request and sends back raw text data, 
universally formatted as JSON or XML, which your application automatically 
interprets and displays.

API key- a code or password for the servers to authenticate you. 
Authentication: It proves to the server exactly who is knocking on the door.
Access Control: It unlocks specific data permissions assigned to your account.
Usage Tracking: It allows the provider to monitor how many requests you make to 
                prevent abuse and calculate billing.

How does it matter for this project -
The NREL NSRDB has API Keys which it gives to the clients for getting data.
The size of these datasets is very big, and sometimes users ask for huge datasets,
even bigger than the API can handle.
So rates also known as API key rate limit is set in order to prevent burdening on 
the server and crashing.

